the input gene list shows a mix of protein-coding genes involved in various cellular processes such as translation (rps*, rpl*), transcription (eef1*, hnrnpa1), metabolism (cox*, mt-), and cell cycle regulation (cdc42, cd3g). the presence of b2m, hla-b, hla-a, and hla-c suggests t cell and b cell activity, and the higher expression of genes involved in ribosome and dna/rna processing (rps*, rpl*) may indicate a cell with high metabolic activity and proliferation rate, similar to plasma cells. rps26, rpl27a, and other ribosomal proteins are found in both plasma and memory b cells, which suggests that the cell is likely a mature immune cell that could be either a plasma or a memory b cell. therefore, compared to the specific gene profile of the plasma cell where cytoskeleton remodelling genes (zyx, cdc42) were up-regulated along with plasma b cell genes (xbp1 was not detected but b2m was), the provided gene signature shows less evidence of plasma cell differentiation and inclines more towards memory b cell where more cd84 and ero1l was absent. (the cell's lack of expression of high plasma cell specific markers such as cd27, hla-dmb, btk, tcl1, and low representation of genes involved in protein modification and energy metabolism may further support this prediction and indicate that it's not a mature plasma cell);
